In this lesson, we'll look at stationary points and how the second derivative helps us figure out exactly what kind of point we've got. A stationary point is simply a spot on a curve where the slope is zero, so the tangent line is flat. To find candidates, you take the first derivative, then set it equal to zero and solve. Whatever x-values pop out are your possible stationary points. Now, there are three types: a local maximum, where the curve peaks, a local minimum, where it dips, and a horizontal point of inflection, where the curve flattens but keeps going the same way. To tell them apart, you take the second derivative and plug in your candidate value. If the second derivative is negative, you're at a maximum, because the curve is bending downwards like a frown. If it's positive, you've got a minimum, bending upwards like a smile. If it equals zero, the test is inconclusive, so you might need to check the third derivative or look at a sign chart. And don't forget, points of inflection can also happen where the second derivative is zero and changes sign, meaning the curve switches from curving up to curving down, even without a flat tangent. Now let's see it in action.